{"id": "b31529ba-2f57-41f0-a39d-feed108fdc17", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T15:33:52.864196", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "8ce835a5-d141-4333-9bf4-a63feb6ab42d", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-a80e3743-7cbd-46a4-ae73-c773ec347db5", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"cat /workspace/task.json 2>&1\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.008, \"output\": \"{\\n  \\\"question\\\": \\\"How critical are dynamic context memory lookup and the choice of unit representations to InfLLM\\u2019s performance across long-context tasks?\\\",\\n  \\\"context\\\": \\\"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\\\",\\n  \\\"documents\\\": {\\n    \\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "5605d3d6-e5f6-4276-bb21-1e359be5fd6d", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 8}
{"id": "d09b343d-e6ee-46c2-99ee-5208bbae0491", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T15:33:52.873007", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "b31529ba-2f57-41f0-a39d-feed108fdc17", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-e7053aca-6b38-4b04-b11c-47e72f81b022", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"file /workspace/task.json 2>&1; echo \\\"---\\\"; wc -c /workspace/task.json 2>&1\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.008, \"output\": \"/workspace/task.json: JSON text data\\n---\\n433 /workspace/task.json\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "8ce835a5-d141-4333-9bf4-a63feb6ab42d", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 9}
{"id": "c29e0a73-39f1-46fe-b9f6-19f45f58e354", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T15:34:38.361839", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "d09b343d-e6ee-46c2-99ee-5208bbae0491", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-6d0d1220-12d0-4c8d-b6bc-1a3418f14031", "tool_call": {"id": "call-6d0d1220-12d0-4c8d-b6bc-1a3418f14031", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Initial inspection: InfLLM dynamic memory, units, sketch\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## 1) Inspection — what I have on InfLLM from memory\\n\\n**Caveat up front:** no InfLLM source / paper / weights are reachable from this sandbox. Everything below is from my prior knowledge of the method, not from inspecting code. I'll flag which points I'm confident vs. fuzzy.\\n\\n### What InfLLM does to memory — \\\"dynamic context memory lookup\\\"\\n- InfLLM is a *training-free* scheme that lets a pretrained short-context LLM (base window typically ~2k–4k) attend over a much longer context at inference time.\\n- It maintains an **external \\\"memory pool\\\"** of KV states evicted from the sliding local window. Things outside the current local window live in this pool instead of being discarded.\\n- At each step, attention is computed over two sources:\\n  1. The **local window** — recent tokens equal to the model's trained context length, with standard (mostly full) attention among themselves.\\n  2. A small number of **selected memory units** pulled in from the pool.\\n- The \\\"dynamic\\\" part: which units get pulled in is decided *per query* (or per query group) by a similarity score between the current query's key and the unit's representation. Usually a top-k retrieval over unit-token similarity, then those units' KVs are concatenated to the local KVs for the attention computation. This is what gives the \\\"dynamic context memory lookup\\\" its name — it's essentially k-NN–style routing on top of attention, not a static lookup table.\\n- Positional encoding of memory units has to be handled consistently with the local window; the paper does something specific (relating a unit's stored position to the block it represents).\\n\\n### Unit representations InfLLM uses\\n- The pool is not token-level — that would blow up memory and selection cost. So the historical context is partitioned into **fixed-size contiguous blocks (\\\"units\\\" / \\\"memory units\\\")**, typically a few hundred tokens each (256 / 512 in my recollection, but I am not 100% sure of the default).\\n- Each unit is compressed into a **single vector per head per layer** for the Key and Value sides used both for storage/retrieval scoring and for attention. The compression flavors I recall being associated with InfLLM:\\n  - **Convolution-based compression unit**: a 1D convolution with kernel = block size, stride = block size, applied over the K (and V) sequence inside a block, producing one vector per block. This is the headline mechanism I associate with InfLLM.\\n  - **Pooling-based unit**: mean / sum pool of token KVs inside a block — a simpler, weaker baseline form.\\n  - I'm less certain whether InfLLM also experiments with **first-token-only** or **attention-pooled** unit representations; treat those as plausible ablation levels, not confirmed as part of the original method.\\n- Query side for scoring may use a representative vector too (e.g., pooled query block) rather than per-token keys.\\n\\n### Where these two mechanisms should matter, in my view\\n- **Dynamic lookup (selection policy)** is most critical when *the right context fragment is not the most recent one*: needle-in-haystack / passkey retrieval, multi-hop QA across distant documents, code-repo QA. It should be less critical for tasks dominated by recency (e.g., simple continuation, short summarization of the last few pages). Replacing it with random-k or recency-k should hurt retrieval-type tasks disproportionately.\\n- **Unit representation** is critical in two coupled ways:\\n  - **What gets selected**: the unit vector used for query–unit similarity drives retrieval; a representation that flattens discriminative signal hurts even a perfect top-k.\\n  - **What gets attended to**: once a unit is in the attention set, the compressed K/V must preserve enough local structure for the model to reason over the block's content — matters for tasks needing fine-grained info (exact-match answers, code, math).\\n- **Interaction**: a stronger representation can partially rescue a weaker selection policy; a strong selection policy on top of a bad representation still misses. So both axes matter, and they have a non-trivial interaction worth probing.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2) Proposed approach — skeleton (bullet sketch, no code)\\n\\n### Research question decomposed\\n- How much of InfLLM's long-context gain comes from **dynamic, query-conditioned lookup** vs. just *having* a memory pool?\\n- How much of it comes from **how each unit is represented** beyond the choice of block size alone?\\n- Do these two mechanisms matter differentially across **task families** (retrieval vs. reasoning vs. summarization vs. code)?\\n\\n### Factors (axes to ablate)\\n- **F1 — Memory/lookup policy** (the \\\"dynamic lookup\\\" axis)\\n  - L1: **None** — pure local-window attention (truncation baseline).\\n  - L2: **Static pool** — attend to *all* memory units (no selection), i.e., nearest-neighbor-free extension.\\n  - L3: **Recency-k** — always pick the k most recently stored units (no query conditioning, current scheme bypassed).\\n  - L4: **Random-k** — pick k units uniformly at random per query (random baseline for selection).\\n  - L5: **InfLLM dynamic** — query-conditioned top-k over unit representations (the actual method).\\n- **F2 — Unit representation kind** (held within the pool)\\n  - L1: Token-level (no compression; pricey, but diagnostic).\\n  - L2: Mean-pool unit (block mean of KVs).\\n  - L3: Conv-based unit (1D conv kernel = block size, stride = block size) — the InfLLM default I recall.\\n  - L4 (probe, not necessarily in the paper): First-token-only unit; possibly an attention-pooled unit.\\n- **F3 — Unit block size / unit grain**\\n  - Sweep e.g. {64, 128, 256, 512} tokens per unit. This interacts with F2 (conv with kernel=N only makes sense if N = block size).\\n- **F4 — Memory budget (top-k once F1 = L5)**\\n  - Sweep k (e.g., 16 / 32 / 64 / 128 selected units), at *matched representation cost* across F2 — i.e., fix total key/value bytes, not raw k, so changing F2 doesn't secretly inflate budget.\\n\\n### Long-context tasks (covering categories where the two mechanisms should diverge)\\n- **Retrieval / exact-match**: Needle-in-a-Haystack (passkey at multiple depths and context lengths); a LongBench retrieval task like HotpotQA or 2WikiMQA.\\n- **Reading comprehension over a single long doc**: NarrativeQA, Qasper (LongBench).\\n- **Multi-hop / multi-doc reasoning**: HotpotQA / MuSiQue under long context (LongBench); a counting/aggregation probe (∞Bench-style) for fine-grained extraction.\\n- **Summarization**: GovReport / SummScreen (LongBench) — recency-dominated control.\\n- **Code understanding**: LCC / RepoBench-P (LongBench) — high locality, fine-grain unit sensitivity expected.\\n- (Optional diagnostic) **Perplexity curves** over increasing context length, as a model-side sanity check independent of any benchmark.\\n\\n### Baselines\\n- **Truncation window** (no memory, F1=L1) — the lower bound.\\n- **StreamingLLM-style** (attention sinks + sliding window) — a non-memory long-context baseline.\\n- **Full attention** on inputs that fit in the trained window — anchor for \\\"no degradation\\\" behaviour.\\n- **F1 landmarks / non-memory long-context methods** — treat as an external comparator if you want a broader map, not strictly required.\\n- **F1 = L3/L4 baselines** (recency-k, random-k) — internal to the ablation and *the* control for isolating \\\"dynamic\\\" specifically.\\n- **F1 = L2 (static pool, all units)** — control for \\\"having memory\\\" without \\\"dynamic lookup\\\".\\n\\n### Analysis plan (sketch, not scripts)\\n- **Main effect of F1**, per task: how much does InfLLM dynamic beat recency-k / random-k / static / none?\\n- **Main effect of F2** at fixed F1 = InfLLM dynamic: which representation is best, on which tasks.\\n- **F1 × F2 interaction**: does a stronger representation reduce the gap between dynamic and random selection? I.e., is dynamic lookup mostly \\\"buying\\\" representation weakness, or vice versa?\\n- **Task-family effect**: plot Δ across task families. Expectation to test (and possibly overturn): lookup dominates on retrieval/reasoning, representation dominates on fine-grain/code/math, and summarization is least sensitive to both.\\n- **Budget sweep (F4) at matched representation cost**: find the per-task knee point, and whether the optimal representation changes with budget.\\n- **Length sweep**: separately run at e.g. 8k / 16k / 32k / 64k to see whether the effects are stable or only appear at long contexts.\\n\\n### Controls / nuisance variables to lock\\n- Same backbone, same checkpoint, same local window size across all cells.\\n- Same total KV memory budget when comparing F2 variants (otherwise representation changes confound budget).\\n- Same generation config, same prompt template, same evaluation harness, same random seeds.\\n- Same depth/position distribution for needle-style probes so retrieval isn't accidentally always \\\"easy depth\\\".\\n- Same set of context lengths across task types, to disentangle task-family effects from length effects.\\n\\n### Feasibility notes I have to flag (not run, but design-relevant)\\n- Reference implementation: I'm assuming access to an existing InfLLM inference path (or willingness to re-implement from the paper). If neither, dynamic-lookup and conv-unit ablations are not faithfully runnable.\\n- Compute: dynamic top-k per-token attention over 32k+ contexts with multiple F2/F4 levels × tasks × seeds is multi-GPU-days at minimum for a 7B-class model; CPU-only sandbox won't run it (and isn't meant to).\\n- Evaluation harness: needs LongBench / ∞Bench / L-Eval / passkey probes plus standard scoring scripts.\\n- Position-encoding handling for memory units is method-specific and must be preserved across F2 variants to avoid a separate confound.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties\\n\\n### Assumptions I'm making (please correct any of these)\\n- A1. InfLLM's default unit is a **fixed-size contiguous token block** (likely 256 or 512 tokens).\\n- A2. The headline compression is a **1D convolution** over K/V with kernel = block size; mean-pool is a reasonable \\\"weaker\\\" comparison point.\\n- A3. Selection is **query-conditioned top-k** over unit vectors, applied per generated token (or per query block) using dot-product similarity.\\n- A4. Dynamic lookup is the load-bearing novelty I should isolate; \\\"static pool\\\" / \\\"recency-k\\\" / \\\"random-k\\\" are the right internal controls.\\n- A5. A 7B-class open backbone with a 2k–4k base window (Llama-2-7B / Mistral-7B family) is acceptable for the ablation; happy to target a specific one.\\n- A6. The interesting exploitation range starts around 8k and goes up — effects of memory lookup on short contexts are likely degenerate.\\n\\n### Unknowns / things I'd need from you before locking the final design\\n- U1. **Backbone**: which model and which base context length?\\n- U2. **Reference implementation**: is there an InfLLM fork we can patch, or do we rebuild from the paper? This directly determines whether F1/F2 ablations are faithful.\\n- U3. **Compute envelope**: how many GPU-hours / which model scale is in scope? Drives whether F4 (budget sweep) and length-sweep cells survive.\\n- U4. **Evaluation suite**: LongBench vs. ∞Bench vs. L-Eval vs. a custom mix? Do you want the same suite across all cells, or per-task-family suites?\\n- U5. **Default block size and k** to anchor the F1=InfLLM-dynamic cell at — i.e., what counts as \\\"the method\\\" when we declare it the reference level.\\n- U6. **Whether positional-encoding handling** for memory units is in-scope as a factor or must be frozen from the paper.\\n- U7. **Whether training-free is hard-fixed**, or whether a \\\"use InfLLM-style memory but fine-tune the conv\\\" auxiliary cell is wanted.\\n- U8. **Scope of \\\"unit representation\\\"** — confirm whether first-token / attention-pooled variants should be included, or only mean-pool vs. conv (and possibly token-level) as I drafted.\\n\\nI'll wait on this batch before turning this into the full experiment design with concrete cells, metrics, and an analysis plan.\",\"evidence\":\"Workspace contents (`ls /workspace`, `find /workspace/task.json`) show only the task metadata; `task.json` itself says \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" No InfLLM source, paper PDF, weights, or datasets are present locally. Network is disabled per the task environment block, so I did not attempt to fetch the official InfLLM repo (e.g., the `InfLLM` GitHub implementation) or arXiv paper at runtime; the inspection above is reconstructed from prior knowledge of the method rather than from any artifact inspected in this session. I did not run, and did not plan to run, any experiments.\",\"assumptions\":\"See block (3) in the answer: A1 fixed contiguous unit blocks; A2 1D-conv compression as InfLLM default; A3 query-conditioned top-k selection; A4 dynamic lookup as the load-bearing novelty to ablate; A5 7B-class open backbone with 2k–4k base window; A6 interesting range from ~8k upward.\",\"uncertainties\":\"See block (3) in the answer, U1–U8: backbone, reference implementation availability, compute budget, evaluation suite, default block size & k, positional-encoding treatment scope, training-free hard constraint, and which unit representations are in-scope.\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-109b1d08