{"id": "f49191be-0dad-4962-b264-0f8815f872dc", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T15:35:12.999241", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "f2c5f6ce-098e-4b15-9929-d1a65b7d8caa", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## 1) Inspection — what I have on InfLLM from memory\n\n**Caveat up front:** no InfLLM source / paper / weights are reachable from this sandbox. Everything below is from my prior knowledge of the method, not from inspecting code. I'll flag which points I'm confident vs. fuzzy.\n\n### What InfLLM does to memory — \"dynamic context memory lookup\"\n- InfLLM is a *training-free* scheme that lets a pretrained short-context LLM (base window typically ~2k–4k) attend over a much longer context at inference time.\n- It maintains an external \"memory pool\" of KV states evicted from the sliding local window. Things outside the current local window live in this pool instead of being discarded.\n- At each step, attention is computed over two sources:\n  1. The local window — recent tokens equal to the model's trained context length, with standard (mostly full) attention among themselves.\n  2. A small number of selected memory units pulled in from the pool.\n- The \"dynamic\" part: which units get pulled in is decided *per query* (or per query group) by a similarity score between the current query's key and the unit's representation. Usually a top-k retrieval over unit-token similarity, then those units' KVs are concatenated to the local KVs for the attention computation. This is the \"dynamic context memory lookup\" — essentially k-NN–style routing on top of attention, not a static lookup table.\n- Positional encoding of memory units has to be handled consistently with the local window; the paper has a specific scheme.\n\n### Unit representations InfLLM uses\n- The pool is not token-level — that would blow up memory and selection cost. Historical context is partitioned into fixed-size contiguous blocks (\"units\" / \"memory units\"), typically a few hundred tokens each (256 / 512 in my recollection, not 100% sure of the default).\n- Each unit is compressed into a single vector per head per layer for the Key and Value sides, used both for storage/retrieval scoring and for attention. Compression flavors I associate with InfLLM:\n  - Convolution-based unit: 1D conv kernel = block size, stride = block size over K (and V), producing one vector per block — the headline mechanism I attribute to InfLLM.\n  - Pooling-based unit: mean / sum pool of token KVs inside a block — a simpler, weaker comparison form.\n  - Less certain whether InfLLM also uses first-token-only or attention-pooled unit representations; treat those as plausible ablation levels, not confirmed parts of the original method.\n- Query side for scoring may use a representative vector (e.g., pooled query block) rather than per-token keys.\n\n### Where these two mechanisms should matter, in my view\n- Dynamic lookup (selection policy) is most critical when the right context fragment is not the most recent one: needle-in-haystack / passkey retrieval, multi-hop QA across distant documents, code-repo QA. Less critical for tasks dominated by recency (short summarization of the last few pages, simple continuation). Replacing it with random-k or recency-k should hurt retrieval-type tasks disproportionately.\n- Unit representation matters in two coupled ways:\n  - What gets selected: the unit vector used for query–unit similarity drives retrieval; a representation that flattens discriminative signal hurts even a perfect top-k.\n  - What gets attended to: once a unit is in the attention set, the compressed K/V must preserve enough local structure for the model to reason over the block's content — matters for tasks needing fine-grained info (exact-match answers, code, math).\n- Interaction: a stronger representation can partially rescue a weaker selection policy; a strong selection policy on top of a bad representation still misses. So both axes matter, and they have a non-trivial interaction worth probing.\n\n---\n\n## 2) Proposed approach — skeleton (bullet sketch, no code)\n\n### Research question decomposed\n- How much of InfLLM's long-context gain comes from dynamic, query-conditioned lookup vs. just *having* a memory pool?\n- How much of it comes from how each unit is represented beyond the choice of block size alone?\n- Do these two mechanisms matter differentially across task families (retrieval vs. reasoning vs. summarization vs. code)?\n\n### Factors (ablation axes)\n- F1 — Memory/lookup policy (the \"dynamic lookup\" axis)\n  - L1 None: pure local-window attention (truncation baseline).\n  - L2 Static pool: attend to *all* memory units (no selection).\n  - L3 Recency-k: always pick the k most recently stored units (no query conditioning).\n  - L4 Random-k: pick k units uniformly at random per query.\n  - L5 InfLLM dynamic: query-conditioned top-k over unit representations (the actual method).\n- F2 — Unit representation kind (held within the pool)\n  - L1 Token-level (no compression; pricey, but diagnostic).\n  - L2 Mean-pool unit (block mean of KVs).\n  - L3 Conv-based unit (1D conv kernel = block size, stride = block size) — the InfLLM default I recall.\n  - L4 (probe): First-token-only unit, possibly an attention-pooled unit.\n- F3 — Unit block size / unit grain\n  - Sweep e.g. {64, 128, 256, 512} tokens per unit. Interacts with F2 (kernel=N only meaningful if N = block size).\n- F4 — Memory budget (top-k) once F1 = L5\n  - Sweep k (16 / 32 / 64 / 128), at matched representation cost across F2 (fix total KV bytes, not raw k), so changing F2 doesn't secretly inflate budget.\n\n### Long-context tasks (covering categories where the two mechanisms should diverge)\n- Retrieval / exact-match: Needle-in-a-Haystack (passkey at multiple depths and lengths); a LongBench retrieval task like HotpotQA or 2WikiMQA.\n- Reading comprehension over a single long doc: NarrativeQA, Qasper (LongBench).\n- Multi-hop / multi-doc reasoning: HotpotQA / MuSiQue under long context; a counting/aggregation probe (∞Bench-style) for fine-grained extraction.\n- Summarization: GovReport / SummScreen (LongBench) — recency-dominated control.\n- Code understanding: LCC / RepoBench-P (LongBench) — high locality, fine-grain unit sensitivity.\n- (Optional diagnostic) Perplexity curves over increasing context length, as a model-side sanity check independent of any benchmark.\n\n### Baselines\n- Truncation window (no memory, F1=L1) — lower bound.\n- StreamingLLM-style (attention sinks + sliding window) — non-memory long-context baseline.\n- Full attention on inputs that fit in the trained window — anchor for \"no degradation\" behaviour.\n- F1 = L3/L4 (recency-k, random-k) — internal controls that isolate \"dynamic\" specifically.\n- F1 = L2 (static pool) — control for \"having memory\" without \"dynamic lookup\".\n\n### Analysis plan (sketch, not scripts)\n- Main effect of F1, per task: how much does InfLLM dynamic beat recency-k / random-k / static / none?\n- Main effect of F2 at fixed F1 = InfLLM dynamic: which representation is best, on which tasks.\n- F1 × F2 interaction: does a stronger representation reduce the gap between dynamic and random selection? I.e., is dynamic lookup mostly \"buying\" representation weakness, or vice versa?\n- Task-family effect: plot Δ across task families. Expectation to test (and possibly overturn): lookup dominates on retrieval/reasoning, representation dominates on fine-grain/code/math, summarization is least sensitive to both.\n- Budget sweep (F4) at matched representation cost: per-task knee point, whether the optimal representation changes with budget.\n- Length sweep: 8k / 16k / 32k / 64k to see whether effects are stable or only appear at long contexts.\n\n### Controls / nuisance variables to lock\n- Same backbone, same checkpoint, same local window size across all cells.\n- Same total KV memory budget when comparing F2 variants (otherwise representation changes confound budget).\n- Same generation config, same prompt template, same evaluation harness, same seeds.\n- Same depth/position distribution for needle-style probes so retrieval isn't accidentally always \"easy depth\".\n- Same set of context lengths across task types to disentangle task-family effects from length effects.\n\n### Feasibility notes (design-relevant, not run)\n- Reference implementation: I'm assuming access to an existing InfLLM inference path (or willingness to re-implement from the paper). Without one, F1/F2 ablations are not faithfully runnable.\n- Compute: dynamic top-k over 32k+ contexts × F2/F4 levels × tasks × seeds is multi-GPU-days at minimum for a 7B-class model; the CPU-only sandbox can't and shouldn't run it.\n- Evaluation harness: needs LongBench / ∞Bench / L-Eval / passkey probes plus standard scoring scripts.\n- Position-encoding handling for memory units is method-specific and must be preserved across F2 variants to avoid a separate confound.\n\n---\n\n## 3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties\n\n### Assumptions I'm making (please correct any of these)\n- A1. InfLLM's default unit is a fixed-size contiguous token block (likely 256 or 512 tokens).\n- A2. The headline compression is a 1D convolution over K/V with kernel = block size; mean-pool is the natural \"weaker\" comparison.\n- A3. Selection is query-conditioned top-k over unit vectors, applied per generated token (or per query block) using dot-product similarity.\n- A4. Dynamic lookup is the load-bearing novelty to isolate; static pool / recency-k / random-k are the right internal controls.\n- A5. A 7B-class open backbone with a 2k–4k base window (Llama-2-7B / Mistral-7B family) is acceptable for the ablation; happy to target a specific one.\n- A6. The interesting exploitation range starts around 8k and goes up — effects of memory lookup on short contexts are likely degenerate.\n\n### Unknowns / things I'd need from you before locking the final design\n- U1. Backbone: which model and which base context length?\n- U2. Reference implementation: is there an InfLLM fork we can patch, or do we rebuild from the paper? This directly determines whether F1/F2 ablations are faithful.\n- U3. Compute envelope: how many GPU-hours / which model scale is in scope? Drives whether F4 (budget sweep) and length-sweep cells survive.\n- U4. Evaluation suite: LongBench vs. ∞Bench vs. L-Eval vs. a custom mix? Same suite across all cells, or per-task-family suites?\n- U5. Default block size and k to anchor the F1=InfLLM-dynamic cell at — i.e., what counts as \"the method\" when we declare it the reference level.\n- U6. Whether positional-encoding handling for memory units is in-scope as a factor or must be frozen from the paper.\n- U7. Whether \"training-free\" is hard-fixed, or whether a \"use InfLLM-style memory but fine-tune the conv\" auxiliary cell is wanted.\n- U8. Scope of \"unit representation\" — confirm whether first-token / attention-pooled variants should be included, or only mean-pool vs. conv (and possibly token-level) as drafted.\n\nI'll wait on this batch before converting this into the full experiment design with concrete cells, metrics, and analysis.", "evidence": "Workspace contents (`ls /workspace`, `find /workspace/task.json`) show only the task metadata; `task.json` itself states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" No InfLLM source, paper PDF, weights, or datasets are present locally. Network is disabled per the task environment block, so I did not attempt to fetch the official InfLLM repo or arXiv paper at runtime; the inspection above is reconstructed from prior knowledge of the method, not from any artifact inspected in this session. No experiment was run, and none is being proposed to run inside this sandbox.", "assumptions": ["A1. InfLLM's default unit is a fixed-size contiguous token block (likely 256 or 512 tokens).</item>\n<item>A2. The headline compression is a 1D convolution over K/V with kernel = block size; mean-pool is the natural 